WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.240
VOICEOVER: In the early hours&nbsp;
of the 24th of February, 1987,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:11.251
astronomers at the Las Campanas observatory in&nbsp;
Chile spotted something astonishing.

00:00:11.251 --> 00:00:14.293
A new bright dot in the sky.

00:00:14.293 --> 00:00:19.608
170 000 years after&nbsp;
exploding in the Large Magellanic Cloud

00:00:19.608 --> 00:00:27.760
just past the edge of our galaxy, the light from&nbsp;
the supernova later named SN1987A reached earth

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:35.400
The chance sighting was soon&nbsp;confirmed
by other astronomers and made news world wide.

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:39.731
The brightest&nbsp;supernova
recorded in nearly 400 years,

00:00:39.731 --> 00:00:43.840
and the closest to be captured&nbsp;
by modern instruments.

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:50.160
Finally scientists had a chance to observe&nbsp;
a supernova at relatively close range,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:52.560
from almost the first moment it appeared,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:58.240
testing years of theorising and fueling&nbsp;
hundreds of papers worth of research.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:06.720
SN1987A taught scientists more about these cosmic&nbsp;
events than any other supernova - but there is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:12.000
still so much more to learn - and capturing&nbsp;
the first moments as a new supernova appears&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:13.257
in the sky could be key,

00:01:13.257 --> 00:01:15.850
which begs the&nbsp;
question -

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:18.469
how will astronomers know where to look?

00:01:18.469 --> 00:01:20.576
And critically,

00:01:20.576 --> 00:01:25.840
make&nbsp;sure they don’t miss the next one?

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:40.320
SN 1987 A was a type 2, core collapse supernova.

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:46.480
when a star at the end of its life, in this&nbsp;
case a blue supergiant called Sanduleak 69 202,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:51.578
is unable to produce enough energy to push&nbsp;
back against its own enormous gravity.

00:01:51.578 --> 00:01:56.479
It starts to contract, and an&nbsp;
iron core forms at its center.

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:03.920
Matter from the core then collapses inwards&nbsp;
even further. In mere milliseconds a burst of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:08.640
neutrinos is released as protons and&nbsp;
electrons are compressed so tightly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:14.080
that they combine to form a proto-neutron&nbsp;
star as dense as the nucleus of an atom.

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:20.480
Meanwhile the intense gravity pulls&nbsp;
the remains of the star onto this core,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:25.280
releasing a shockwave that rebounds&nbsp;
outwards, with such force that new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:31.120
heavy elements are produced and expelled as the&nbsp;
star disintegrates in the supernova explosion.

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:39.280
The light from a single supernova can be as&nbsp;
bright as entire galaxies, but this represents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:45.360
only a small fraction of the total energy emitted&nbsp;
- the rest was carried away by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:53.040
the near massless, barely interacting neutrinos.&nbsp;
And that provides scientists with an opportunity.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:03:00.160
KATE SCHOLBERG: The neutrinos actually get out&nbsp;
of the supernova before the light does and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:05.680
doesn't mean neutrinos travel faster than light&nbsp;
the reason is that it takes quite a while for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:10.880
photons to beat their way out of the envelope&nbsp;
because photons interact a lot neutrinos don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:17.520
they just sail out and so the neutrinos will come&nbsp;
out promptly and we will get a burst and observe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:24.320
the burst on Earth before the the light would&nbsp;
give us a first, a first signal of the supernova.

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:27.920
VO: This is Kate Scholberg,&nbsp;
she is part of a group that set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:32.000
up the Supernova Early Warning System or SNEWS,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:38.240
an international collaboration aimed at catching&nbsp;
the next big supernova in unprecedented detail.

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:41.600
KATE: That's the name Supernova Early&nbsp;
Warning System the neutrinos give us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:46.720
an early warning that a supernova may be&nbsp;
about to appear somewhere in the galaxy

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:51.440
VO: SNEWS links together the world’s&nbsp;
neutrino detector experiments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:56.640
so that bursts of supernova neutrinos&nbsp;
can be quickly identified and reported.

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:02.240
KATE: If any two or more of those&nbsp;
detectors send a message that's time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:10.480
tagged within 10 seconds then snooze will send&nbsp;
out an alert to the to the astronomical community.

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:14.240
VO: And that gives astronomers a vital head start.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:18.640
Telling them to search the skies in the hopes&nbsp;
that they will detect the moment that light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:24.640
from a supernova first reaches earth, hot on the&nbsp;
heels of the neutrino burst and rich with data.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:31.600
SNEWS has been online for decades and has never&nbsp;
triggered an alert, and now it's due an upgrade,

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:35.520
KATE: I mean it is very simple and robust and&nbsp;
actually that's an advantage it's basically just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:41.840
run perfectly well almost no downtime over&nbsp;
more than decades so that's the advantage of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:47.520
being simple but being simple means also&nbsp;
you don't have potentially full capacity

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:52.880
VO: The SNEWS team wants to make the network&nbsp;
more sensitive. To avoid false alarms the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:57.680
threshold for an alert has always been set&nbsp;
very high, but as the years have gone on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:02.160
astronomers have become accustomed to&nbsp;
receiving alerts from networks like SNEWS&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:07.840
and investigating them even if they turn out to&nbsp;
be nothing, and that offers new opportunities.

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:13.680
ALEC HABIG: One of the big things we would&nbsp;
like to do with a revamp a SNEWS 2.0 is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:21.360
be able to have less, less gold-plated&nbsp;
input to to the to the network&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:26.800
if we'd be okay chasing after something that&nbsp;
might, might be supernova neutrinos might not be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:31.280
and people aren't upset about it we'd&nbsp;
like to do that from our own perspective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:35.760
then we get to practice we get to get good at it&nbsp;
we get to make sure there aren't any bugs lurking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:41.600
from the science perspective maybe there&nbsp;
are things that make bursts of neutrinos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:46.400
that aren't quite as blindingly obvious&nbsp;
as a whole star going kaboom in our galaxy.

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:52.800
VO: And that isn’t all - neutrino detectors have&nbsp;
come a long way since SNEWS was first launched

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:56.640
ALEC: They're bigger, their signal is stronger,&nbsp;
they would see more neutrinos because they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.640 --> 00:06:03.200
just physically larger and in the next few years&nbsp;
even larger experiments are coming online and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:10.880
possibly now and certainly within the next decade&nbsp;
each individual's experiment detection of that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:15.760
supernova wavefront of neutrinos sloshing across&nbsp;
their detector they'll be able to get a really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:23.600
good time of that wave past my detector to within&nbsp;
a millisecond and be able to report that time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:31.120
and so by getting measurements of individual&nbsp;
times that experiments down precisely enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:36.000
with today's experiments you could reconstruct&nbsp;
the fact that oh that it went it first went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:41.600
through Italy and then, and then that's&nbsp;
Antarctica and then Japan and use the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:44.640
that you know how fast they're going nearly&nbsp;
the speed of light and the distances between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:50.160
those detectors to be able to say so therefore&nbsp;
it, it came from over there and again get a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:54.920
direction on it which of course is very useful&nbsp;
if you want people to go chase after it right.

00:06:54.920 --> 00:06:59.680
VO: If SNEWS can identify an area of sky to&nbsp;
look at, the world’s telescopes will have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:05.200
a better chance of catching the Supernova -&nbsp;
and that doesn’t only include multi-million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:09.760
dollar observatories. They want all the&nbsp;
world’s astronomers to join the search.

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:14.320
ARTO OKSANEN: I'm Arto Oksanen&nbsp;
from Finland amateur astronomer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:16.320
interested about transient events.

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:20.640
VO: Amateur astronomers like Arto&nbsp;
are a key part of the search.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:26.320
Their excellent knowledge of the night sky&nbsp;
and flexibility to react quickly to alerts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:32.800
make them the perfect resource to pinpoint a new&nbsp;
object, and they have the advantage of numbers.

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:40.400
ARTO: We amateur astronomers we are worldwide&nbsp;
everywhere we can we can jump to any, any object&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:50.000
or target at any time so I, if I get the email&nbsp;
from, from some professional astronomers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:56.720
midnight when i'm observing I can stop what i'm&nbsp;
doing at that time and jump on on the new targets.

00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:03.600
VO: Alerts from SNEWS bring them into the heart&nbsp;
of an international astronomy collaboration.

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:10.000
ARTO: It's the most&nbsp;interesting and
gratifying thing to do&nbsp;so

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:23.040
I have been kind of wishing to be a a professional astronomer myself or scientist but&nbsp;now I can, I can be a scientist on my free time.

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:28.960
VO: Observing the next supernova in our galaxy&nbsp;
will involve a global collective effort,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:33.280
huge neutrino experiments and networks&nbsp;
of astronomers will point the way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:37.520
but understanding these stellar explosions&nbsp;
will need many different perspectives.

00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:42.480
ALEC: In the past it's been I have a telescope&nbsp;
and I can see light of different colors but it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:49.280
still all light and then as humanity got radio&nbsp;
telescopes for example hey you can see radio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.280 --> 00:08:54.640
waves you can see past a lot of stuff you can see&nbsp;
infrared or ultraviolet or x-rays uh gamma rays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.640 --> 00:08:57.760
you can see all these different wavelengths&nbsp;
they're still all electromagnetic radiation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:02.480
but kind of the different categories of them are&nbsp;
so different that that's they're all different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.480 --> 00:09:05.840
messengers even though they're really all&nbsp;
just photons of electromagnetic radiation

00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:10.480
KATE: But there's actually more than that there's&nbsp;
more than photon messengers in the, in the universe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:13.406
neutrinos of course they're&nbsp;
my favorite one

00:09:13.406 --> 00:09:18.560
neutrinos also bring information there's also&nbsp;
gravitational waves and that's a newcomer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:22.560
and then there's also cosmic rays and so&nbsp;
it's really you know much broader than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:26.480
photons multi-messenger astronomy is you know&nbsp;
everybody bringing information to the party.

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:33.040
VO: This is multi-messenger astronomy, the study&nbsp;
of all the signals arriving on earth from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:39.280
cosmic event like a supernova, and it offers our&nbsp;
best chance yet of understanding our universe.

00:09:39.280 --> 00:09:42.320
ALEC: And when you have different&nbsp;
messengers and you can see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:46.880
different aspects of the same process you can&nbsp;
learn a lot more than just looking through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.880 --> 00:09:50.960
whatever small hole you're looking through in&nbsp;
the fence, no matter how cool a picture you get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:54.880
being able to have that different perspective of&nbsp;
different totally different processes making these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.880 --> 00:09:59.280
different messengers either different wavelengths&nbsp;
of light or particles or gravitational waves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:03.840
really lets people pin down what&nbsp;
really is going on out there.

